[TEXT] **Found it.** Direct confirming evidence on `mswitch1`:

```
eth0.10@eth0: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>  state DOWN   master br_vlan10   ← FAULT
eth0.20@eth0: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP>  state UP  master br_vlan20   ← healthy (VLAN 20)
```

The physical link `mswitch1.eth0` (trunk to switch1) is **UP**, and `eth0.20` (VLAN 20) is **UP** — which is why `laptop0` on VLAN 20 works. But **`eth0.10` — the VLAN 10 subinterface on that same trunk — is DOWN** (`<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` flags only, no `UP`/`LOWER_UP`). This severs the VLAN 10 segment of the trunk, making mswitch1's `br_vlan10` bridge deaf to any ARP from pc4/pc5 side.

This matches the link-fault fingerprint exactly (interface DOWN on the owning device, single-VLAN scope explains why VLAN 20 is unaffected). Advancing to Phase 5.

